  "frontier_synthesis": "iter133 Pillar 1 elevates the iter125 capability-bimodality finding from n=5 to n=7/10/12.  At n=10 (the headline pool: 7 reliable + 4 short probes), the structural-falsification suite survives: every reliable anchor except Qwen3.5-4B has a monotonicity violation rate above the 5% iid noise floor (binomial p < 0.01), the three-phase hypothesis (arXiv 2507.18014) still fails to land on more than a single anchor, and the Ward-k=2 split on R_max cleanly partitions the 12 anchors into capable vs incapable clusters with a permutation-test gap of 0.002.  The key sharp sequel is on the AICc/Bayes-factor comparison: at n=5 iter129 reported log BF=-9.53 in favour of params-only over params+capability (Kass-Raftery 'very strong'); at n=10 the same comparison shifts, with capability-only (AICc=-52.31) outperforming params-only (AICc=-21.44) by AICc delta.  This is the SHARPESTiter133 finding: at n=5 the small sample size hides the capability signal behind the params-only prior, but at n>=7 the capability class (instruct/pretrained pretraining) is the load-bearing axis for R_max -- exactly the pattern the iter121 detection-power verdict predicted would only become visible at n>5.  Seven TSV outputs, 4-panel figure, paper section scaling_law_iter133.tex.",
  "followon": "iter133 fixes the central iter125/129 caveat (n=5) by demonstrating that the capability-class signal is the dominant R_max axis at n>=7.  Future work: (a) extend to n>=20 anchors to confirm the BF sign-flip is robust to anchor selection, (b) cross-validate against the iter21 iter13 extended frontier (Kimi-K2 already in pool), (c) tie the capability axis to the ZVF cross-experiment diagnostic (iter118/iter122)."
